text:
If you'll sing song you go along,
In the face of the real the fancied wrong;
In spite Of the doubt, If you'll fight it out,
And show heart that is brave and stout;
If you'll laugh at the jeers and refuse the tears,
You'll force the ever-reluctant cheers
That the world denies when coward cries,
To give to the man who bravely tries;
And you'll win success with little song
If you'll sing the song you go along.
If you'll sing song you plod along,
You'll find that the busy, rushing throng
VIII catch the strain of the glad refrain;
That the sun will follow the binding rain;
That the clouds will fly from the blackened sky;
That the stars will come out by and by,
And you'll make friends, till hope descends
From where the placid rainbow bends;
And all because of little song
If you'll sing the song you plod along.
II you'll sing song as you trudge along,
You'll see that the singing will make You strong;
And the heavy load and the rugged road,
And the sting and the stripe Of the tortuous


gOad


Will soar with the note that you set afoat;
That the beam will change to trifing mote;
That the world is bad when you sad.
And bright and beautiful when glad;
That all you need is little song
If you'll sing the song you trudge along.


Philadelphia Telegraph.

